+/// Where one current ref's element is, for `get_browser_state`'s locate
+/// mode. It proves the binding and the ref's document as a click does and
+/// refuses a stale or removed node the same way, then only reads: the
+/// node's rectangle and the hit test at its centre (script on the node,
+/// the click sequence's own geometry), whether a clipping ancestor hides
+/// part of it, its document's visibility state, whether it has focus, the
+/// hit test at `point` when one is given, the border-box centre from
+/// `DOM.getBoxModel`, and the viewport's zoom and scale. Nothing is
+/// scrolled, focused or dispatched.
+///
+/// The script runs in an isolated world of the tab's main frame
+/// ([`LOCATE_WORLD`]), with the node resolved into that world. The world
+/// shares the page's DOM and none of its script objects, so what the read
+/// calls (`elementFromPoint`, `getBoundingClientRect`, `getComputedStyle`,
+/// `contains`, `isConnected`, `visibilityState`, `hasFocus`) is the
+/// browser's own whatever the page's script replaced. Without such a world
+/// there is no position to report.
+async fn locate_browser_ref(
+    engine: &Arc<BrowserEngine>,
+    session: &str,
